  <article class="prose">
    <h1>How to read a card</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Each published company is one operating bet you can compare — not a
      transcript, and not a compliment sheet.
    </p>

    <h2>The twenty-second test</h2>
    <p>
      Read the thesis. If it could describe almost any SaaS company, the card
      failed and should stay off the map. A good thesis names a specific choice:
      who decides, what they optimize, or what they refuse to do.
    </p>

    <h2>Fields</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Thesis</strong> — the one operating choice that distinguishes this
        org from peers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Context</strong> — what they sell and roughly what stage they’re
        in.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Operating loop</strong> — the concrete cycle they run (diagram or
        bullets). Generic Discovery → Delivery skeletons don’t count.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Ownership</strong> — who discovers, prioritizes, ships, and where
        sales or design sit.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Evidence</strong> — short attributed quotes plus a link to the
        interview.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Gaps</strong> — what the interview doesn’t cover. Absence is part
        of the map.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Statuses</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Published</strong> — on the public map and compare tool.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Weak</strong> — briefed but too soft or not a stable product org;
        kept for rework.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Stub</strong> — extract exists; brief pending or failed.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Craft lean</h2>
    <p>
      Some cards carry a curated craft lean — product engineering, PM craft,
      design-led, or founder craft — only when ownership is clear. Unset means
      the interview doesn’t land the axis. See{" "}
      <a href="/tensions/product-engineering-vs-pm">that tension</a> and the
      full unset list on <a href="/gaps#craft">/gaps</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How to use the atlas</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/">Map</a> — search or filter by stage/craft; click graph
        nodes.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/tensions">Tensions</a> — curated pairs that disagree; the atlas
        as argument.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/pole">Pole</a> — five forced choices that land you on a tension.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/compare">Compare</a> — stack two or three theses side by side.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/desk">Desk cards</a> — print opposing bets for a strategy table.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/gaps">Gaps</a> — what we hold weak, leave unset, or haven’t
        argued yet.
      </li>
      <li>
        Company pages — full loop, ownership, evidence, and source interview.
      </li>
    </ul>

